        "explanation": "Explanation:\nTest case 111: Consider choosing X=9X = 9X=9. Then, f(X)f(X)f(X) will be computed as follows:\n\nWe start with Y=9Y = 9Y=9.\nAt i=1i = 1i=1, we have Y>B1=5Y \\gt B_1 = 5Y>B1​=5.\nSo, we set Y=B1=5Y = B_1 = 5Y=B1​=5.\nAt i=2i = 2i=2, we now have A2≤Y≤B2A_2 \\le Y \\le B_2A2​≤Y≤B2​ because A2=2A_2 = 2A2​=2 and B2=6B_2 = 6B2​=6.\nSo, YYY remains unchanged.\n\nThe final value of YYY is 555, and so f(9)=5f(9) = 5f(9)=5.\nIt can be proved that this is the largest possible value of f(X)f(X)f(X) for any input XXX.\nTest case 222: Because A3=B3=2A_3 = B_3 = 2A3​=B3​=2, no matter which XXX we choose we will always end up with f(X)=2f(X) = 2f(X)=2.\nSo, the answer is 222.\nTest case 333: One possible optimal choice of XXX is X=1X = 1X=1, which can be verified to give f(X)=7f(X) = 7f(X)=7.\nNo other XXX results in a larger f(X)f(X)f(X), so the answer is 777."
